  "text": "proprium.dominica-viii-post-pentecosten-evangelium",
  "about": "The evangelium of the formulary “8th Sunday after Pentecost” in the 1962 Roman Missal.",
  "segments": {
    "s01": { "translation": "At that time, Jesus told His disciples this parable: “A certain man was rich, and he had a steward; and this steward was accused before him of having wasted his master’s goods. And he called him and said to him: ‘What is this I hear about you? Render an account of your stewardship, for you will no longer be able to act as steward.’ Then the steward said within himself: ‘What am I to do, since my master is taking the stewardship from me? I lack the strength to dig; I am ashamed to beg. I know what I will do, so that when I have been removed from the stewardship, they may welcome me into their homes.’ So, calling together each of his master’s debtors, he said to the first: ‘How much do you owe my master?’ And he said: ‘A hundred casks of oil.’ And he said to him: ‘Take your bond, sit down quickly, and write fifty.’ Then he said to another: ‘And you, how much do you owe?’ He said: ‘A hundred measures of wheat.’ He said to him: ‘Take your bill, and write eighty.’ And the master praised the steward of iniquity, because he had acted prudently; for the sons of this world are more prudent in their generation than the sons of light. And I say to you: make friends for yourselves out of the mammon of iniquity, so that, when you have failed, they may welcome you into the eternal tabernacles.”" }
  },
